{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-77680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $x=5$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $x=5$ passes through the points $A(5,0)$ and $B(5,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(-5,0)$ and ${B}^{′}(-4,1).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{1-0}{-4-(-5)}(x-(-5)) \\\\ y & =x+5 \\\\ x-y+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9974f5324429a17f7489734597819e89bf56de56adc0da00283f8fdca36de4cc"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5x-y+10=0"] ["math" "x-5y+1=0"] ["math" "x-y+5=0"] ["math" "x=15"] ["math" "y=5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-y+5=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=2x-3$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=12.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=2x-3$ passes through the points $A(0,-3)$ and $B(1,-1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(0,-6)$ and ${B}^{′}(2,-1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{-1+6}{2-0}(x-0) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{5}{2}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+6 & =\\frac{5}{2}x \\\\ 2y+12 & =5x \\\\ 5x-2y & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9974f5324429a17f7489734597819e89bf56de56adc0da00283f8fdca36de4cc"} {"question" "q-281140" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281140" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5x-2y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=15.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(3,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(16,7).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{7-(-1)}{16-2}(x-2) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{8}{14}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =\\frac{4}{7}(x-2) \\\\ 7y+7 & =4x-8 \\\\ 4x-7y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9974f5324429a17f7489734597819e89bf56de56adc0da00283f8fdca36de4cc"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x-7y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=-1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=-1$ passes through the points $P(0,-1)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(0,2)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,3).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{3-2}{4-0}(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-8 & =x \\\\ x-4y & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9974f5324429a17f7489734597819e89bf56de56adc0da00283f8fdca36de4cc"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-4y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=3$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=21.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=3$ passes through the points $P(0,3)$ and $Q(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(9,3)$ and ${Q}^{′}(10,1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1-3}{10-9}(x-9) \\\\ y-3 & =-2(x-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-2x+18 \\\\ 2x+y & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9974f5324429a17f7489734597819e89bf56de56adc0da00283f8fdca36de4cc"} {"question" "q-281581" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"} {"question" "q-281581" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x+y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b2228107c8443b80282acb07e2f39a659a1740deab716fa4c8e2b557f28f59"}] "usage" {"q-77680" []} "basis" 981 "retractions" [[17592186152695 :question/problem "Consider the line $x = 5$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186151081 17592186151082 17592186151083 17592186151084 17592186151085] "immutable_fields" [[17592186151080 :answer-field/correct] [17592186151080 :answer-field/id] [17592186151080 :answer-field/key] [17592186151080 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
